\use{document}
\use{music}

\title{The \texttt{music} Package}
\author{Simple staff notation, drawn from the document language}
\maketitle

\section{Reading a score}

The \texttt{music} package draws a line of staff notation on a treble clef. A score is
a list of notes: a letter \texttt{a}–\texttt{g} for the pitch, optional \texttt{'} or
\texttt{,} to raise or lower it an octave, and an optional duration digit — \texttt{1}
whole, \texttt{2} half, \texttt{4} quarter (the default), \texttt{8} eighth. A \texttt{|}
is a barline. The heads, clefs, flags, rests, accidentals and time-signature figures are
real engraved glyphs from Bravura, the reference font for music notation; the stems, beams,
staff lines and ledger lines are ruled over the \texttt{\\picture} layer.

\section{A scale}

The C major scale, an octave of quarter notes from middle C — which sits on its own
ledger line below the staff — up to the C above.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c d e f g a b c'}}

\section{Durations}

The same C, held for a whole note, a half, a quarter, an eighth, and a sixteenth. The
longer notes have open heads; the stem appears at the half note, a flag at the eighth, and
a second flag at the sixteenth.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c1 c2 c4 c8 c16}}

\section{Dots}

A dot after the duration lengthens a note by half. It is drawn just right of the head, in
the space above when the note sits on a line. Here a dotted half, a dotted quarter paired
with the eighth that completes its beat, and a run of beamed sixteenths.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c2. d4 | e4. e8 f4 g4 | [ c16 d16 e16 f16 ] g4 a2}}

\section{A melody}

The opening of \emph{Mary Had a Little Lamb}, with barlines and a half note to close
each phrase.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{e d c d | e e e2 | d d d2 | e g g2}}

\bigskip
And the start of \emph{Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star}.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c c g g | a a g2 | f f e e | d d c2}}

\section{Range and ledger lines}

Notes far above or below the staff hang on short ledger lines, drawn only as far as the
note reaches.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c, e, g, c g c' e' g'}}

\section{Accidentals}

An accidental leads a note: \texttt{+} for a sharp, \texttt{-} for a flat, \texttt{n} for
a natural. The sign is drawn just left of the head; it changes how the note is read, not
where it sits, so \texttt{+f} and \texttt{f} share a line. Here a chromatic climb, then a
sharpened note cancelled by a natural.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c +c d -e e f +f g | +f nf f2}}

\section{Beams}

Eighth notes in a run are usually joined by a beam rather than each carrying its own flag.
Bracket a group with \texttt{[} and \texttt{]} and the notes between share one beam.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{[ c d e f ] [ g a b c' ]}}

\bigskip
A beamed phrase mixed with a longer note — the opening of \emph{Ode to Joy}.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{e e f g | g f e d | c c d e | [ e d ] d2}}

\section{Time signatures}

Setting \texttt{\\set musictimenum} and \texttt{\\set musictimeden} draws a time signature
after the clef. Three-four time, a waltz, with the beats grouped into bars.

\bigskip
\set musictimenum {3}
\set musictimeden {4}
\centerline{\score{c e g | c' g e | d f a | g2 e}}
\set musictimenum {}

\section{Key signatures}

A key signature follows the clef. Set \texttt{\\set musickey} to a signed count: a positive
number draws that many sharps, a negative one that many flats, each in its conventional order
and on its conventional line. Here two sharps (D major), then three flats (E-flat major).

\bigskip
\set musickey {2}
\set musictimenum {3}
\set musictimeden {4}
\centerline{\score{d e +f g | a b +c' d' | b a g2}}
\set musictimenum {}
\bigskip
\set musickey {-3}
\centerline{\score{c d e f g a b c'}}
\set musickey {0}

\section{Repeats and barlines}

Besides the plain barline \texttt{|}, a score can write a double barline \texttt{||}, a final
barline \texttt{|.}, and the start and end of a repeated section, \texttt{|:} and \texttt{:|}.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{|: c d e f | g a b c' :| d' c' b a || g1 |.}}

\section{Articulations}

A mark after a note tells the player how to attack it: \texttt{>} an accent, \texttt{!} a
staccato, \texttt{=} a tenuto, \texttt{*} a marcato, \texttt{;} a fermata. Each sits on the
side away from the stem, and several may stack. Here the same phrase plain, then accented,
staccato, tenuto and marcato, closing on a fermata.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c d e f | c> d> e> f> | c! d! e! f! | c= d= e= f= | g2*;}}

\section{Slurs and ties}

A run bracketed by \texttt{(} and \texttt{)} is drawn under one slur. A trailing \texttt{-}
ties a note to the next of the same pitch, holding it across. Here a slurred turn, then two
tied half notes.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{( c d e f g ) a | c'-2 c'2}}

\section{Dynamics and hairpins}

A token beginning \texttt{!} is a dynamic, set below the staff: \texttt{!p}, \texttt{!mf},
\texttt{!ff}, \texttt{!sfz}. A \texttt{<} opens a crescendo hairpin and \texttt{>} a
diminuendo, each closed by \texttt{=}. Here a phrase that starts soft, swells, and falls away.

\bigskip
\centerline{\score{!p c d < e f g a = !f b c' > b a g f = !p e2}}

\section{The bass clef}

Setting \texttt{\\set musicclef \{bass\}} notates in the bass clef instead, where middle
C sits one ledger line \emph{above} the staff. Here a C major scale from the C below the
staff up to middle C.

\set musicclef {bass}
\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c, d, e, f, g, a, b, c}}

\section{Alto and tenor clefs}

The C clef names the line that is middle C: the middle line in the \texttt{alto} clef, the
fourth line in the \texttt{tenor}. The same C major scale around middle C, read in each.

\set musicclef {alto}
\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c d e f g a b c'}}

\set musicclef {tenor}
\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c d e f g a b c'}}

\section{Choosing the music font}

The glyphs come from a SMuFL music font, and \texttt{\\set musicfont} chooses which one. The
default is Bravura, the standard reference font. Setting it to \texttt{petaluma} switches to a
handwritten, jazz-lead-sheet style — the same notes, a different hand. Here the C major scale
again, in Petaluma.

\set musicclef {treble}
\set musicfont {petaluma}
\bigskip
\centerline{\score{c d e f g a b c'}}
